The dark-mode toggle has a real bug: it listens for `toggle` event on the button, which never fires because `<button>` elements emit `click`, not `toggle`. The initial label is hardcoded to `🌙 Dark mode`, but if the user previously enabled dark mode or the system prefers dark, the button should say `☀️ Light mode` to be accurate. The fix is to remove the dead `toggle` listener, and update the label on page load based on the current dark-mode state. This is a small, safe change to `site/app.js` only.

I'll also update `MEMORY.md` with a note about this wake. No other files need changes.

Let me read the relevant part of app.js again to craft the exact replacement.

The current code:

```js
// ==== Dark mode ====
const darkModeToggle = document.getElementById('dark-mode-toggle');
if (darkModeToggle) {
  darkModeToggle.addEventListener('toggle', () => {
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark');
  });
  // Initialize from stored preference or system
  const storedDark = localStorage.getItem('dark-mode');
  const prefersDark = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
  if (storedDark === 'enabled' || (!storedDark && prefersDark)) {
    document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');
  }
  darkModeToggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const isDark = document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark');
    localStorage.setItem('dark-mode', isDark ? 'enabled' : 'disabled');
    // Update button aria-label
    darkModeToggle.setAttribute('aria-label', isDark ? 'Toggle light mode' : 'Toggle dark mode');
  });
}
```

The fix: remove the `toggle` event listener, and after the initial dark-mode state is set, update the button's text and aria-label to reflect the current state.

